# Playwright conventions

**At a glance:** (1) role-first locators — (2) web-first waiting — (3) POMs without asserts — (4) storageState setup auth — (5) unique self-cleaning data — (6) one tag per `test()` — (7) factories, enums, env — (8) API for setup/teardown — (9) mock dependencies not the SUT — (10) axe + keyboard — (11) web-first asserts — (12) isolation over retries.

Examples use illustrative UI names only; confirm real BuddyTime copy in P7 against the live app.

## 1. Locators

Prefer locators that match how users and assistive tech perceive the UI: role and accessible name first; CSS/XPath only when nothing user-facing fits and expect review. When you need the visible match only, chain `locator.visible()` (Playwright 1.63)—not the CSS `:visible` pseudo-class.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save draft' }).click();
await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save draft' }).visible().click();
await page.getByLabel('Event title').fill('Tuesday playdate');
await page.getByText('Invite sent').waitFor();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.locator('div.modal footer button.primary').click();
await page.locator('button:visible').click();
await page.locator('//button[contains(@class,"save")]').click();
```

**Why:** Role and name track user-visible behavior; `.visible()` filters without brittle CSS/XPath that breaks when markup changes.

## 2. Waiting

Use Playwright’s auto-waiting and `expect(locator)`; never sleep or assert on one-off DOM reads.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Dashboard' })).toBeVisible();
await expect(page.getByLabel('Search')).toBeEnabled();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.waitForTimeout(3000);
expect(await page.getByText('Loaded').isVisible()).toBe(true);
```

**Why:** Fixed delays flake; snapshotting `isVisible()`/`textContent()` races the UI instead of waiting for a stable condition.

## 3. Page Object Model

One class per page or component; expose locators from the constructor; methods perform actions only—assertions stay in specs.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
export class EventEditorPage {
  readonly titleField = this.page.getByLabel('Event title');

  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {}

  /** Fills the event title and saves. */
  async saveDraft(title: string): Promise<void> {
    await this.titleField.fill(title);
    await this.page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save draft' }).click();
  }
}
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
async saveAndAssert(title: string): Promise<void> {
  await this.page.locator('#title').fill(title);
  await expect(this.page.locator('.toast')).toHaveText('Saved'); // assert in POM
}
```

**Why:** POMs model *what the user can do*; specs own *what should be true*, keeping reuse and failure messages clear.

## 4. Auth

Log in once per family in the `setup` project; persist `storageState` to `AUTH_FILE` (main) and `ALT_AUTH_FILE` (second parent). Two-family flows use a second context—never sign in inside each test.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
// auth.setup.ts — runs in setup project
await page.goto('/login');
await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(process.env.APP_USER_EMAIL!);
await page.getByLabel('Password').fill(process.env.APP_USER_PASSWORD!);
await page.context().storageState({ path: AUTH_FILE });

// spec — second family
const alt = await browser.newContext({ storageState: ALT_AUTH_FILE });
const altPage = await alt.newPage();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('parent@example.com');
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill('hunter2');
});
```

**Why:** Setup auth is fast and stable; per-test login adds flake, duplicates secrets, and fights parallel runs.

## 5. Test data

Every test creates unique data and cleans up; no shared ordering or leftover state in the shared test environment.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test('creates invite @smoke', async ({ page, request }) => {
  const title = `Playdate ${Date.now()}`;
  const id = await createEventViaApi(request, { title });
  try {
    await page.goto(`/events/${id}`);
    await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: title })).toBeVisible();
  } finally {
    await deleteEventViaApi(request, id);
  }
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test('uses the default Tuesday event', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/events/default-tuesday'); // assumes another run created it
});
```

**Why:** Parallel CI and shared tenants collide when tests reuse fixed names or depend on someone else’s data.

## 6. Tags

Exactly one tag per `test()`: `@smoke` (PR), `@sanity` (push), `@regression` (manual full suite), `@api`, `@e2e`, `@destructive` (only shared/global state both families need—run with `--workers=1`). Never tag `describe()`. Revert mutating tests in `afterEach`/`afterAll`.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test('home loads @smoke', async ({ page }) => { /* … */ });

test('site-wide banner @destructive', async ({ page }) => {
  /* mutates org settings both families share */
  try { /* … */ } finally { await restoreOrgDefaults(); }
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test.describe('Invites', { tag: '@smoke' }, () => {
  test('sends invite', async ({ page }) => { /* … */ });
});

test('my profile photo @destructive', async ({ page }) => {
  /* only this user’s avatar — should be own-data + cleanup, not destructive */
});
```

**Why:** Tags drive CI selection; `@destructive` serializes truly shared state—misuse hides flake and blocks parallel safe tests.

## 7. Test data sources

Build dynamic values in `test-data/factories/` (Faker + `Date.now()`), static invalid sets in `test-data/*.ts`, secrets/URLs from `process.env`, repeated strings in enums (e.g. `test-data/routes.ts`).

✅ **Good**

```typescript
import { buildFamilyInvite } from '../test-data/factories/invite.factory';
import { AppRoute } from '../test-data/routes';

const payload = buildFamilyInvite({ suffix: String(Date.now()) });
await page.goto(AppRoute.InvitesNew);
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.goto('https://hardcoded-host.example/app/invites/new');
await page.getByLabel('Email').fill('fixed.user+1@test.com');
```

**Why:** Central factories and enums keep data valid and DRY; env and routes avoid hardcoded hosts and credentials.

## 8. API

Use the `request` fixture for setup, teardown, and contract checks; don’t walk the UI to seed data when an API exists.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
test('shows created event @api', async ({ page, request }) => {
  const { id } = await request.post('/api/events', { data: buildEventBody() }).then(r => r.json());
  await page.goto(`/events/${id}`);
  await expect(page.getByRole('heading', { name: /Playdate/ })).toBeVisible();
  await request.delete(`/api/events/${id}`);
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test('shows event', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/events/new');
  /* 12 UI steps to create the same record the API could create in one call */
});
```

**Why:** API setup is faster, clearer, and less brittle than duplicating create flows in every spec.

## 9. Network mocking

Use `page.route` / `route.fulfill` for dependency failures (500, 503, timeout, empty list, bad JSON)—never mock the endpoint the test is meant to exercise.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/notifications**', route =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 503, body: 'Service Unavailable' }),
);
await page.goto('/dashboard');
await expect(page.getByText('Notifications unavailable')).toBeVisible();
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/events/123**', route =>
  route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ title: 'Fake' }) }),
);
await page.goto('/events/123'); // asserting behavior of a faked SUT response
```

**Why:** Mock upstream failures to test resilience; faking the SUT hides integration bugs you meant to catch.

## 10. Accessibility

Run `@axe-core/playwright` with `wcag2a` / `wcag2aa` on key pages; add a keyboard path; prefer `getByRole(..., { name })` so names are real.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
import AxeBuilder from '@axe-core/playwright';

test('settings a11y @regression', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/settings');
  const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page }).withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa']).analyze();
  expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
  await page.keyboard.press('Tab');
  await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Save changes' })).toBeFocused();
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
test('looks fine', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/settings');
  await expect(page.locator('.settings-panel')).toBeVisible(); // no scan, no keyboard
});
```

**Why:** Automated WCAG checks plus keyboard flows catch issues role locators alone might miss.

## 11. Assertions

Default to web-first `expect(locator)`; use `expect.soft` for multi-check pages; `toHaveScreenshot` only with dynamic regions masked.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
await expect(page.getByRole('alert')).toHaveText('Invite sent');
await expect.soft(page.getByRole('listitem')).toHaveCount(3);
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('dashboard.png', {
  mask: [page.getByText(/Good (morning|afternoon)/)],
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
const text = await page.locator('.alert').textContent();
expect(text).toContain('Invite sent');
await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('dashboard.png'); // clock/date shifts every run
```

**Why:** Locator assertions retry; soft asserts collect failures; masked screenshots avoid time-based flake.

## 12. Anti-flake

Rely on isolation, pinned locale/timezone (see config), and `page.clock` for time-sensitive UI; in CI use `retryStrategy: 'isolated'` (1.62) so retries get a clean environment. Tests that touch one shared resource should use `test('…', { lock: '<resource>' }, …)` (1.63)—not a whole tag slice with `--workers=1`. Retries and trace-on-first-retry are safety nets, not substitutes for fixing root cause.

✅ **Good**

```typescript
// playwright.config.ts
retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0,
retryStrategy: 'isolated',

test('slot list respects today @e2e', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2026-06-15T10:00:00-04:00') });
  await page.goto('/availability');
  await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Today, Jun 15' })).toBeVisible();
});

test('org banner @destructive', { lock: 'org-settings' }, async ({ page }) => {
  /* only one test holds this lock at a time; other workers keep running */
});
```

❌ **Bad**

```typescript
// playwright.config.ts — “fixing” flake
retries: 5,
workers: 1, // entire @destructive slice serial; prefer per-test lock when one resource conflicts
```

**Why:** Isolated retries and locks narrow contention; global `workers: 1` slows the whole suite while hiding shared-state bugs.

## Refusals (hard stops)

Never weaken or delete an assertion to pass; never heal a real bug; never replace a role locator with CSS/XPath; never use `waitForTimeout`, a hardcoded credential, or the `any` type. Each one is a conflict the agent raises, not a preference it can be talked out of.
